<figure class="vector-figure">
 <svg viewBox="0 0 330 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="vector-title vector-description">
  <title id="vector-title">Four points and a query at one, zero</title><desc id="vector-description">All third coordinates are zero. Point 1 coincides with the query at 1,0. Point 2 at .9,.1 is closer than point 3 at .8,.2, but belongs to tenant 8. Points 1,3 and 4 belong to tenant 7. Point 4 is at 0,1.</desc>
  <path d="M40 30V240H275" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><path d="M40 40L240 240" stroke="currentColor" stroke-dasharray="3 6" opacity=".35"/>
  <g fill="currentColor" font-size="12"><text x="24" y="258">0</text><text x="235" y="282">x = 1</text><text x="3" y="22">y = 1</text></g>
  <g fill="#648b87" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"><circle cx="240" cy="240" r="6"/><circle cx="200" cy="200" r="6"/><circle cx="40" cy="40" r="6"/></g><rect x="214" y="214" width="12" height="12" fill="#b28b56" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/>
  <g fill="currentColor" font-size="12"><text x="248" y="260">1 = query</text><text x="235" y="216">2 · tenant 8</text><text x="205" y="185">3 · tenant 7</text><text x="53" y="44">4 · tenant 7</text></g>
 </svg>
 <figcaption>All four vectors have a third coordinate of zero, so this plane shows their Euclidean distances exactly. The closest eligible two are 1 and 3. Choosing the closest two first gives 1 and 2; removing tenant 8 then leaves only 1.</figcaption>
</figure>
